1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,830
Business Insider ran this headline:

2
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:04,520
"Companies laying off staff

3
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:06,320
this year include Meta,

4
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:07,080
Amazon,

5
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:08,190
and Dropbox."

6
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:11,630
Laid off and fired mean different things.

7
00:00:11,630 --> 00:00:12,510
Laid off:

8
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:14,440
the company cut your job.

9
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:14,990
Fired:

10
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:16,660
the company cut you.

11
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:18,740
So in an interview say:

12
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:19,800
"I was laid off in

13
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:21,160
a round of cuts."

14
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:22,360
One more thing.

15
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,030
The noun is LAY-off.

16
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:26,590
The phrasal verb is to lay OFF.

17
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:29,390
If you're upper-intermediate or advanced,

18
00:00:29,390 --> 00:00:31,540
and you want more speaking practice,

19
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:32,990
join The English Edge,

20
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:34,760
my conversation community.

21
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:36,660
Try it free for 7 days.

22
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,380
Link in the post.
